<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Con O'Neill of Clandeboye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1619">1619</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Con O'Neill of Clandeboye was the last Gaelic lord of Upper Clandeboye in County Down, Ireland. Imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrickfergus">Carrickfergus</a> in 1602, he escaped with the help of the Scottish laird Hugh Montgomery, who in return obtained a share of his lands. By 1605 most of his territory had passed to Montgomery and James Hamilton, opening north Down to Scottish settlement before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>.</p></div>
